[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"CSS Modules: Scoped Styles in React"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"CSS Modules are a popular and effective way to manage CSS styles in React applications. They solve the problem of global CSS scope by automatically generating unique class names for your CSS rules. This ensures that styles defined in one component don't accidentally affect other components, leading to more maintainable and predictable code."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Use CSS Modules?"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Local Scope: Styles are scoped to the component they are defined in, preventing naming collisions and unintended style overrides."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Explicit Dependencies: CSS Modules make it clear which styles are used by a component, improving code readability and maintainability."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dead Code Elimination: Tools can analyze your code and remove unused CSS rules, reducing the size of your CSS files."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Composability: CSS Modules allow you to compose styles from multiple files, making it easier to reuse and share styles across your application."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Up CSS Modules in a React Project"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Most modern React project setups (created with Create React App, Next.js, or similar tools) already support CSS Modules out of the box. If you're using a custom setup, you might need to configure your build process (e.g., using Webpack) to handle CSS Modules. This tutorial assumes you have a project setup that supports CSS Modules."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a CSS Module"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a CSS Module, simply create a CSS file with the `.module.css` extension (or `.module.scss`, `.module.less`, etc., depending on your preprocessor). For example, let's create a `Button.module.css` file:"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":".button {
  background-color: #4CAF50;
  border: none;
  color: white;
  padding: 15px 32px;
  text-align: center;
  text-decoration: none;
  display: inline-block;
  font-size: 16px;
  cursor: pointer;
}

.button:hover {
  background-color: #3e8e41;
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Importing and Using CSS Modules in a React Component"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In your React component, import the CSS Module. The import will resolve to a JavaScript object where the keys are the class names you defined in the CSS file, and the values are the unique, generated class names.  You can then use these class names in your JSX."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';
import styles from './Button.module.css';

function Button(props) {
  return (
    <button className={styles.button}>{props.children}</button>
  );
}

export default Button;"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `styles.button` will contain the unique class name generated for the `.button` class in `Button.module.css`.  This ensures that the button's styles are scoped to this component and won't conflict with styles defined elsewhere in your application."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Multiple Classes"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can apply multiple CSS classes to an element by concatenating the class names.  Use template literals for a cleaner approach."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';
import styles from './Button.module.css';

function Button(props) {
  return (
    <button className={`${styles.button} ${props.className}`}>{props.children}</button>
  );
}

export default Button;"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we're combining the `button` class from the CSS Module with any additional classes passed as a prop to the `Button` component. This allows for greater flexibility and customization."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Composing Styles"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"CSS Modules also allow you to compose styles from other CSS Modules or global CSS files. This is useful for reusing styles and creating a consistent look and feel across your application. Use the `composes` keyword."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":".base {
  font-family: sans-serif;
  font-weight: bold;
}

.primary {
  composes: base;
  color: white;
  background-color: blue;
  padding: 10px 20px;
}"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `.primary` class composes the styles from the `.base` class. This means that the `.primary` class will inherit the `font-family` and `font-weight` properties from the `.base` class, in addition to defining its own `color`, `background-color`, and `padding` properties."},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"To compose styles from a different CSS Module, specify the path to the module:"},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":".myClass {
  composes: button from './Button.module.css';
  color: red;
}"},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Global Styles"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes you need to define global styles that apply to the entire application (e.g., resetting default browser styles).  You can do this by creating a regular CSS file (without the `.module.css` extension) and importing it into your main application file (e.g., `index.js` or `App.js`)."},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"/* global.css */
body {
  margin: 0;
  font-family: sans-serif;
}"},{"order":30,"type":"code","text":"// index.js
import React from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom/client';
import App from './App';
import './global.css';

const root = ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById('root'));
root.render(
  <React.StrictMode>
    <App />
  </React.StrictMode>
);"},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Using CSS Modules"},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improved Code Organization: CSS Modules encourage you to organize your styles in a modular way, making it easier to understand and maintain your codebase."},{"order":33,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduced CSS Size: By eliminating unused CSS rules, CSS Modules can help reduce the size of your CSS files, leading to faster page load times."},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enhanced Collaboration: CSS Modules make it easier for multiple developers to work on the same project without worrying about style conflicts."},{"order":35,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"CSS Modules provide a powerful and effective way to manage CSS styles in React applications. By scoping styles to individual components, they prevent naming collisions and improve code maintainability. With their composability features and support for global styles, CSS Modules offer a flexible and scalable solution for styling React applications of any size."}]
